WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.340
- Hi, everyone, this is Priya Natarajan,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.870
the current director of the Franke Program

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.240
in Science and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.820
I'm absolutely delighted to

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.690
invite you all

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.690
to our convening and distinguished
speaker series today.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.590
And the title of the talk
today is A Better Planet:

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.650
40 Big Ideas for a Sustainable Future.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.840
But before I tell you about
our distinguished guest,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.710
let me start by thanking our benefactors,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.880
Richard and Barbara Franke,
for their generosity

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.930
in funding this program that enables

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.600
all the exciting activities
that we are able to do.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.350
And I'm really grateful that you know,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.640
our activities are such
up in such a format

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.330
that in this moment of pandemic disarray,

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.280
we are able to continue.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.280
And I am very sorry that
we are not able to offer

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.190
the wonderful receptions,
in-person receptions,

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:10.240
the food and drink that go with

00:01:11.943 --> 00:01:14.320
the celebratory atmosphere.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.100
But however,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.693
we are going to make do with
intellectual excitement.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.240
So first I need,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.780
all of you who are assembled

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.740
that we are recording this event,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.260
and that all participants must
therefore mute their videos,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.450
'cause then we would have to get

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.140
individual permissions and so on.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.163
So kindly mute your videos.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.520
Wish you could submit talks
through the chat feature.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.000
This is the process we
followed previously.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:44.940
And

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.840
Ty will help us moderate this

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.370
and thank you so much Ty for doing that.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:51.700
So,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.710
our speaker today, I'm
thrilled to introduce

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.750
one of our own distinguished
faculty members,

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.490
Professor Dan Esty.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.590
Dan Esty is the Hillhouse
Professor at Yale University

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.980
with primary appointments
at Yale's Environmental

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.760
and Law schools and a
secondary appointment

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.510
at the Yale School of Management.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.460
He currently serves as the director

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.010
of the Yale Center for
Environmental Law and Policy,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.310
co-director of the Yale
Initiative on Sustainable Finance.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.710
Current research and writing
offer cutting-edge thinking

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.950
about climate change,
sustainable investing,

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.830
innovation in the energy
environment context.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.710
And of course, matters of
corporate sustainability,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.380
and brand new approaches,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.490
radical new approaches to
environmental protection.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:44.390
He's a pioneering thinker
in helping us reshape

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.580
how we should think about climate change

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.180
and how to alleviate it.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.280
He is the author and editor
or editor of 12 books

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.140
and dozens of articles on
environmental protection,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.450
regulatory reform, energy policy
and sustainability metrics

00:02:58.450 --> 00:02:59.360
and their connections

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.100
to corporate strategy competitiveness.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.663
So very much rooted in the
policy in the real world.

00:03:04.663 --> 00:03:05.940
He had a prize winning,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.210
he's had many prize winning books.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.350
His latest book, a 2019 book,

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.780
in which he has edited a
wonderful set of essays.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.683
And I would like to highly recommend that.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.610
I thoroughly enjoyed reading it.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.810
And Dan just mentioned that
it's just come out in paperback.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:29.130
So I really urge you all
to buy it and read it.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.530
So this book was just awarded

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.130
the Financial ft Book of the Year Award

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.930
and his previous prize winning
volume was "Green to Gold:

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.960
How Smart Companies Use
Environmental Strategy to Innovate,

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.270
Create Value, and Build
Competitive Advantage".

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.010
And this has recently been named

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.780
one of the top green business
books of the past decade.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.030
And he has had many interesting articles.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.530
I would like to point one out that I think

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.697
was particularly impactful for me,

00:03:57.697 --> 00:03:59.190
"Red Lights to Green Lights:

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.440
From 20th Century Environmental Regulation

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.610
to 21st Century Sustainability".

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.210
So I wanted to say a little
bit about his career trajectory

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:10.440
only because of how distinguished it is

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.870
and how varied it is.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.780
And it gives you a sense of someone

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.660
who really crosses
disciplinary boundaries.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.460
That is a hallmark of what we celebrate

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.990
at the Franke Program.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.057
From 2011 to 2014, Professor Esty served

00:04:24.057 --> 00:04:26.530
as commissioner of
Connecticut's Department

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.470
of Energy and Environmental Protection,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.160
where he earned a reputation
for fresh thinking

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.010
and practical results.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.500
His policy innovations,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.280
he launched many policy innovations,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.970
include the launch of Connecticut's

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.350
first-in-the-nation Green
Bank to promote clean energy

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:46.070
using limited public funding
to leverage private capital

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.690
and the "LEAN" restructuring

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.920
of all of Connecticut's
environmental permitting programs

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.460
to make the state's
regulatory framework lighter,

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.470
faster, and more efficient
as well as effective.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.080
Prior to taking up his
professorship at Yale in 1994,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.810
he served in a variety of senior positions

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.880
at the U.S. EPA where he helped manage

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.670
the agency's regulatory programs.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:10.150
So you can see that he
has had enormous impact

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.800
both in the policy realm with action,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.620
as well as intellectual
heft with his ideas.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.460
He's also worked as a senior fellow

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.650
at the Peterson Institute

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.320
for International Economics
in Washington, D.C.,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.100
where he did breakthrough
work on the links

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.200
between trade and environmental issues

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.610
and the broader challenges
of globalization.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.830
So before we head into today's talk,

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.370
I just wanted to remind
you of two upcoming events,

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.420
the next two monthly talks,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.350
just want to briefly advertise them.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.710
The talk for November is going to be

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.660
the Mapping as Knowing series.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.560
And for that, the speaker for that,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:50.670
we managed to snag

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:55.160
the famous science fiction writer, and,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.195
thinker,

00:05:56.195 --> 00:05:58.850
author of the "Anthropocene",
Kim Stanley Robinson.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:03.100
And our December edition of
the Mapping as Knowing talk

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.870
is gonna be given by Frank Snowden

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.540
who has worked on epidemics.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:12.440
And so anyway, without further ado, Dan,

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.180
we are delighted to have you here

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.810
and look forward to hearing from you.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.780
Thank you so much for
accepting our invitation.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.370
- Priya, thank you so
very much for having me.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:26.240
And special thanks to Ty Kamp
who organized today's program,

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.990
and to Barbara Franke who
makes this all possible.

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.710
So I'm grateful.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.530
And really thank you to all
of you who are taking time out

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.760
of your busy day to be
part of this program today.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.180
I'm gonna present for a bit
but very much hope to move

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.600
to a conversation and a
back and forth dialogue,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.290
in the not too far future.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:49.030
So let me start by saying that
it's just a great pleasure

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.020
to have a chance to talk about
this "Better Planet" book,

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.830
especially in the week that
it comes out in paperback.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.750
I hadn't planned it this
way but it's great timing.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.430
And for those who are
interested there is in the chat

00:07:03.839 --> 00:07:06.110
a brochure with a discount coupon.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.460
So if anyone finds themselves
motivated to go out

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.830
and buy the book, Yale University Press

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.020
will make it worth your
while with the coupon.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:14.853
So they're in fact,

00:07:14.853 --> 00:07:16.360
I think two of the brochures of the book,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.720
one of them has a coupon attached.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.860
Now I wanna start by saying

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.027
that it's actually a
incredibly interesting moment

00:07:24.027 --> 00:07:27.550
and one might even say a critical
moment for the discussion

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.560
that I wanna have about
a sustainable future,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:31.830
including climate change,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.540
but also addressing a
broader set of issues.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.500
And I say that, not just because

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.540
we're sitting here on the East
Coast, at least some of us

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.660
while the West Coast is on fire,

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.990
California has been burning.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.520
But we've also seen hurricanes pylon

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.420
one on top of another into the Gulf Coast.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.210
Both of these elements of climatic danger

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.190
are I think signals,

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:59.730
not necessarily of any
specific climate change

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:02.890
we can link up in a very
tight causal connection,

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:05.110
but in the kind of world
that climate scientists

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.470
tell us we should anticipate
in the years ahead.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:11.670
And I do think the
threat of climate change,

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.820
and the broader challenge
to sustainability

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.550
is become more and more evident.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:20.180
It's become a question in
our political dialogue.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.640
Certainly in the election campaign,

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.320
these issues are playing a
much more prominent role.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.900
But I really actually wanna
say that what's special

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.000
about the moment from my point of view,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.210
is that we are about to
shift gears as a nation.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.160
Next month, we shift maybe not in the day

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.090
after the election but by the
end of November, I'm hopeful

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:40.270
that we shift from all
politics all the time,

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.450
to questions of policy.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:46.120
And that's where this
book really has its place.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.877
It is, as you heard, subtitled,

00:08:48.877 --> 00:08:52.320
"40 Big Ideas for a Sustainable Future".

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.380
It is a volume that I edited,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.600
but represents 56 different
authors in these 40 essays,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.160
offering up a range of perspectives,

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.570
from a range of political starting points,

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:06.810
on a range of underlying disciplines,

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.870
including some folks who are practitioners

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:13.220
and not academics, but all
offer interesting perspectives

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:17.470
on critical issues, and
thoughtful, carefully constructed,

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.100
analytically rigorous pathways forward.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.980
And that's what I'm actually very excited

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.610
to dig into a bit today.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:27.210
The book is, by the way
from Yale University Press.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.340
A great partnership
with the longtime editor

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.320
there who's done science and environment,

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.780
Jean Thomson Black.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:35.640
So really just a huge pleasure

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.440
to have been able to work with her

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.170
and to bring this volume to
publication in record time.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:45.800
And it grows out of an effort
that the environment school

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:47.520
by the way, those who have been following

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.800
him for a long time may not
have kept up with the fact

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.300
that the Yale School of Forestry
and Environmental Studies

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.260
has rebranded itself as
of a couple of months ago

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.890
as the Yale School of the Environment.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.150
And the new dean a few
years ago, Indy Burke

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.930
launched the school on an effort

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.850
to rethink its direction
and strategic plan.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:09.040
And one of the things that
emerged was a commitment

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.700
to be more of a convening platform

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.350
to bring people together to
talk about sustainability

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.387
and how to get to a sustainable future.

00:10:16.387 --> 00:10:20.180
And this book really grows
out of that broader dialogue,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.720
the Yale environmental
dialogue, as we call it,

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.620
organized by Dean Indy Burke,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.840
and now kind of a core part
of the school's program.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.860
And the book itself offers

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.280
a range of concepts and principles

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.530
for thinking about
environmental challenges,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:40.100
and related clean energy
questions, land use issues,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.370
natural resource management,

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.940
so the full spectrum of
sustainability issues.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.410
It offers frameworks, it
looks at technologies,

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:49.720
it looks at pathways and strategies.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:54.720
So it really is an interesting
compilation of ideas.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.030
By the way, it represents
a number of authors

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.900
from within the school of the environment,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.160
others from across the
Yale campus more broadly,

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.570
some number of alums as well.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.810
And three essays are written by students

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.750
who were able to win the
chance to be in the book

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.893
based on a very competitive process.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:18.003
So let me offer you some thoughts.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:23.320
And I wanna start by saying
that the idea of my chapter

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:28.310
is that we need to imagine
a different way of thinking

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:30.720
about the environment in the 21st century

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.950
from what we did in the 20th century.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.890
So that's the core
hypothesis I'm introducing.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.440
That what we did in the past,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.090
particularly as we launched the modern era

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:42.570
of environmental protection
in the United States,

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:46.710
in the 1970s and in the
1980s, worked at the time,

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.860
served the purpose of that era,

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.150
but it's not serving us so well today,

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.660
and that what we really
need is a fresh approach,

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:57.170
a new approach that advances our strategy

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:01.723
and gives us a more clear
path to a sustainable future.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:06.410
My essay in the book is called
"Red Lights to Green Lights:

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:10.200
Toward an Incentive Oriented
Sustainability Strategy".

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.620
And a greater focus on incentives,

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:15.870
on how we change behavior,
change company behavior,

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.460
particularly the big
polluting industry behavior.

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:21.410
But how we change individual
choices and behavior

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:25.130
is really, I think, a fundamental
issue and a real concern

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:28.380
that needs to be a focus
in environmental policy.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:29.770
So that's my own essay.

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:33.410
But I'm also building on a
need for better incentives

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:36.720
to engage people in delivering
the results we need.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:40.340
More use of information
and information technology,

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.640
which of course wasn't
available in the same way

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.160
in the 20th century
when our modern efforts

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.820
in environmental protection were launched.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:50.270
I also, having spent a lot
of time trying to understand

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.870
what's worked and what hasn't
over the last 50 years,

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:57.480
believe that we need to put
much more focus on investment,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.530
how we flow money to solve
environmental problems

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.070
and put us toward a sustainable future.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:06.910
I think we underattended to
that problem in the 1970s, '80s.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.890
Somehow there was an idea
that if the law was framed,

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.390
the money would follow,
turned out not to be true.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:17.150
I also think there is a need
to focus on integration,

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.100
on a more systems-oriented approach,

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.520
we might call it a more
ecological approach

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:22.930
to these problems.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.700
And I'm gonna dig in on all of this,

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:28.140
over the course of the
next 20 minutes or so,

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.000
as I lay out my argument for why we need

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.017
a 21st century approach to
environmental protection,

00:13:34.017 --> 00:13:36.970
and how this "Better
Planet" book sketches,

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.230
not only in my essay,
but in all the essays,

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.730
some of the elements of
what that might look like.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:45.630
So first of all, I wanna be
clear that I'm not saying

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:48.360
that the 20th century strategy

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.390
of environmental protection was a failure.

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:54.180
Quite to the contrary, we
have better quality air,

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.210
now much better quality rivers.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:57.420
For those who pick up the book,

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.830
you'll see that the introductory chapter,

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:01.420
I note that I grew up along

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.570
the Naugatuck River in
Northwestern Connecticut.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:07.300
And that river used to
flow orange and purple,

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.420
depending on the colors
of the unit oil factory

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.160
that was dumping its waste into the river.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:14.630
The river is much cleaner today,

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:16.950
it's swimmable, there's fish in it.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:20.300
So we have made huge
progress as a society.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.020
But I do think they're important questions

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:25.410
about how we continue to
make progress and address

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:28.980
the residual problems that
continue to plague us.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.620
And that's where my argument focuses

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.460
on the need for innovation.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:35.550
My belief is we've gotten a good distance

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.610
with a certain set of strategies,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.390
but to go where we need to
go over the coming years,

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:43.990
we need new ideas, new
approaches, new technologies

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:46.610
to be brought into the
sustainability world.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:51.610
And my basic claim is that
the 20th century approach

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.590
to environmental protection
was largely around telling

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:59.380
people, particularly the
business world, what not to do.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:02.600
So putting up a red light, a
stop sign saying don't do that,

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.530
don't pollute, can't go there.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.430
And that sort of red light strategy

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:10.330
did change behavior in some regards

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:13.860
but it was not very
good at getting business

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.060
to become solutions providers.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:19.580
So we don't have a
transformed energy foundation

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:22.840
for our society compared to
what we thought we might have

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:26.430
in the way of transformation
over the past 50 years.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:28.990
So I wanna argue that we should have,

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:31.160
just as we have at any intersection,

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.570
not just red lights that
tell people when to stop,

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.070
but green lights that
tell people when to go,

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:39.580
we need the same framework of
green lights as a complement

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:42.820
to red lights in environmental regulation.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.480
And what I mean really is
that we need incentives,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.730
we need to get people
who have the capacity

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:51.740
to solve problems, to
step up to those issues,

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.030
to become solutions providers.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:57.430
And I think my argument is that
we need to really structure

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:01.060
incentives to flow society's resources

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.220
towards those solutions providers.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:05.700
And frankly, to steer
money away from companies

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.100
that are cause of problems

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:11.130
and are not moving us
towards a sustainable future.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.710
So here's the issues that I see us

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:17.340
potentially being able to step up to.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:19.780
First, I think there is a value

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:23.150
in making people pay
for the harm they cause.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:26.930
So the biggest green light is
to have those who are doing

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:30.580
things in a clean way,
not polluting, not pay

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:35.240
and that's a signal of strong
value in the business world.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:39.960
So again, my number one
green light is price signals.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:41.120
And that would tell us

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.250
if you're doing things the
right way, you don't pay,

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:46.010
if you're a polluter, you're
gonna pay for every increment

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.910
of harm you're putting out into society.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.700
And this is best done if we switch

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:55.150
from the 20th century approach
to environmental protection.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:57.020
It was largely what we in the business

00:16:57.020 --> 00:17:00.500
call command and control regulation

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:03.420
largely around government
mandates that you can't exceed

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.980
a certain level of pollution,
but everyone was allowed

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:08.250
to pollute up to that level for free.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.610
My argument is nothing for free anymore.

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:13.210
Either you stop your harm, no pollution,

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:15.040
in which case you don't have to pay.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:16.270
But if you're continuing to pollute,

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:18.260
you're gonna pay for
every incremental harm

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.680
up to the limit beyond
which it's forbidden.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:24.040
And so that's the core
idea of a price signal.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:28.260
By the way, in the "Better
Planet" book, Bill Nordhaus,

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:29.410
our prize winning,

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.960
Nobel Memorial Prize
Winning Economics colleague

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:35.100
lays out the case for price signals

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.670
as the core of the response
to climate change and I agree.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:41.990
Now more broadly, my argument

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:43.437
is that we should really seek to,

00:17:43.437 --> 00:17:45.660
and this is the radical
part of the agenda,

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:49.160
Priya promised that I'm
working on some radical ideas.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.870
The big one is that we should
really be committed to ending

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:55.430
what economists would call externalities.

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:58.680
Smoke up a smokestack,
effluent out of pipeline,

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:01.700
any kind of spillover
of harm from an industry

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.990
or from an individual
onto society more broadly.

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:06.770
So I think every company should pay

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:08.810
for its emissions out the smokestack,

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:12.060
every one of us should
pay for the car exhaust

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:13.990
at the back of our car.

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.230
And I think it was not possible to do

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.770
this in the 20th century,

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:20.610
because it was much too
complicated to keep track of.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.840
But our information age makes
that tracking of pollution

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:24.990
and the pricing of harms

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:27.240
and the passing out of pollution bills,

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.480
really not a very
difficult exercise at all.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.840
And that again, if we make
people pay for the harm

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:34.670
they're causing or stop what they're doing

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:36.610
that's causing that problem,

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.150
that internalizes the externality,

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:41.820
make sure that no one
is getting the benefit

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:45.310
of putting harms out
onto society for free.

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:48.440
By the way, this will
change some business models

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:50.770
dramatically, because
there are some companies

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:53.790
that couldn't survive, except for the fact

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.430
they are privatizing the
gains of their activities,

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.950
the products they sell, but
externalizing the costs.

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.140
Putting at least in part, those burdens,

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:05.740
whether in the form of
pollution or something else

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.823
on to the community, on to
the society more generally.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.410
So I am arguing

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.370
that we need a new
Environmental Protection Agency,

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.770
not spending its time with
engineers figuring out

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:20.630
what the best available technology is

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:22.700
and that's the core of our current law,

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.990
and ordering various industries to apply

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:26.510
a certain smokestack scrubber

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:28.970
or other piece of
pollution control equipment

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:31.200
onto their factory activity,

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:34.920
but rather to have an EPA
focused on measuring harm,

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.810
putting a price on it, by
calculating how much risk

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:42.750
and epidemiological or
ecological harm is being caused,

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.570
and then sending out the bills,

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:47.130
making people pay for what they're doing.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.170
I think that kind of a structure creates,

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:53.010
again, a big incentive
to control pollution.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:55.050
So every factory would
have a big incentive

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:59.240
to reduce its harm, but also
to entrepreneurs and innovators

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.100
to provide new approaches to doing

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.570
all the things we need to do,

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.500
that will be much lower emissions.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.750
They will be the ones to
bring to the factories

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.100
ways to cut their harm

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.350
and therefore cut their pollution bills.

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:15.610
So I think what it does is
it incentivizes innovation,

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:19.500
and drives people to
become solutions providers.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:23.010
Now, again, as I said, this
connects in a number of ways

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:26.300
to some of the other essays
that are in this book.

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.810
Ken Gillingham, my colleague
in the environment school,

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:32.070
one of the rising star, young
environmental economists,

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:33.770
also makes a big argument

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.950
around the importance of innovation.

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:38.210
So I'm excited that his essay lines up

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.320
so nicely with my own.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:41.680
And of course, as I mentioned,

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:43.680
Bill Nordhaus, the same thing.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.590
He's making an argument
around the importance

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.890
of a policy framework that
puts a price on causing harm.

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:52.800
And his central focus, as I mentioned,

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.510
is on a carbon charge, making
people pay for the greenhouse

00:20:56.510 --> 00:21:00.270
gas emissions that are
contributing to climate change.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:02.070
But there are a lot of other ideas

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:04.150
that I wanna just mention.

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.030
And I guess, I wanna pause and say

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.380
that both the Ken Gillingham idea

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.400
and the Bill Nordhaus idea,

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.670
are aimed not only at
changing the behavior

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.750
of the polluter, but also inspiring others

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.380
to help polluters stop polluting.

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:21.900
So it's really about not
just incentivizing more care

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.360
with the production activities of society,

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:28.110
but also to spur innovation and investment

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:33.060
in energy efficiency, in
clean energy technology,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.680
in renewable power generation.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:37.910
So again, if that is what can be done

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:39.380
without paying a price,

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.900
there will be a relative price
signal to steer people away

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:46.280
from fossil fuel burning
towards these new cleaner,

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:48.123
more renewable technologies.

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:52.730
Another source of green
lights can be information.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:56.930
So another part of the 21st
century sustainability strategy

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:00.370
that I wanna sketch out
for you is how we use

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:04.090
data and information and the
capacity to get our hands

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:06.060
on that information, manage it,

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.670
and make it actionable
with information technology

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:13.300
in ways that were unimaginable
a couple of decades ago.

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.310
And in our "Better Planet"
book are several essays

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:20.060
that talk about how to do
this in one way or another.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.730
One of the areas that I've been working

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:24.900
on through the Yale Initiative
on Sustainable Finance,

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:26.060
which I co-direct

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.400
with my school of management
colleague, Todd Cort,

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.970
is centered on better metrics

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.550
of corporate sustainability performance.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:37.320
So asking companies to give us

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:42.310
more data on their environmental,
social and governance

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.090
performance in the marketplace.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:46.600
And that is talked about
in the investment world

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:50.340
by its acronym, ESG reporting.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:52.260
And right now, some companies report

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.900
but the data is not very good.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:56.670
A lot of its backward
looking not forward looking,

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:58.740
a lot of its narrowly focused on risk

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.130
and not always focused on
who's a solutions provider,

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:03.360
therefore, upside opportunities,

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:05.500
as well as some of the downside burdens

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:07.080
the company is causing.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:09.780
A lot of it is very focused on reputation,

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:12.720
rather than operational success.

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:16.640
So there's a lot of problems
with an existing framework.

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:18.780
So Todd and I have been
arguing in his essay

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:20.910
in the "Better Planet" book makes clear

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:24.930
that we would benefit as a
society from better metrics,

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.570
better data on corporate performance.

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.250
Really trying to understand

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:32.340
who the corporate
sustainability leaders are,

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:35.960
and hold them up as a place
that investors might wanna go

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:39.400
and what we have found is
that an ever larger number

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:42.810
of investors are sustainability minded.

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:45.640
They want better alignment
between their values,

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:48.460
and where their portfolios
are putting their money.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:51.760
So this idea of better ESG metrics

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:55.300
is very much what they
need to make this desire

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:57.200
for an alignment between their values

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:59.670
and their portfolio come together.

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:01.750
So I think that's a great example

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:04.840
of where better data
investment and information

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:09.840
and in this case specifically
in a next generation of ESG

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.753
metrics is going to be essential.

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:17.600
Now, of course, some of its
still will be risk-oriented.

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:19.560
And from a climate change point of view,

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:24.320
knowing which companies are
as we now say, carbon exposed,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:26.500
likely to be suffering more consequences

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:28.380
than their competitors or those companies

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:31.857
that are in other industries
will be of real value.

00:24:31.857 --> 00:24:35.720
And in particular, companies
holding fossil fuel assets

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:40.040
may find themselves with what
are known as stranded assets;

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:42.800
oil in the ground, natural gas underground

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.160
that can't ever be taken advantage of,

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:47.790
because it is become disfavored

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.450
as we move to a clean energy future.

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:53.400
But the next generation of
metrics that I wanna see

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:56.110
goes even beyond those
kind of core elements,

00:24:56.110 --> 00:25:00.310
the things like which
companies and which industries

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:03.000
and which management
teams of those companies

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:07.280
are positioned to transition
to a decarbonized future?

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:08.220
Because that's what people

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:10.360
would really like to
know in the marketplace.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:13.080
Looking out ahead 10 or 15 or 20 years,

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:17.000
who is positioned to be
a success in business,

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.320
and whose business model may fail,

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:23.313
because it's trapped in
the fossil fuel past?

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:27.580
Now, I think that whole
idea of a different approach

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:29.950
for business of really
trying to understand

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:33.120
who the leaders are from a
sustainability perspective,

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:35.340
dovetails with another of the essays

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:39.060
that I very much like,
the one by Carter Roberts,

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:41.830
who's the head of the World Wildlife Fund.

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.630
And he has written about kind of the need

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:46.890
for a new business ethos,

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:50.060
and that actually is capturing
a spirit of the moment.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:52.120
Suddenly, there's a lot of talk,

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.673
that the old business mission of thinking

00:25:54.673 --> 00:25:57.890
that the goal is always
to maximize profits,

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:01.130
or to deliver what the
famous University of Chicago

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:04.750
economist once called shareholder primacy,

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:07.740
is giving way to a new business spirit

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:12.350
that focuses on
stakeholder responsibility.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:16.700
And that means that a business
is not just delivering goods

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:19.630
and profits for its
shareholders, its owners,

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:22.120
but it has some responsibility
to its employees,

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.960
to the communities it operates in,

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:26.330
to its customers, to its suppliers.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:30.430
And I think that argument very
much is what Carter Roberts

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:34.260
is saying in his essay in
this "Better Planet" book.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:37.510
And I believe that there
is a societal value change

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:41.090
underway that is basically
saying to the business community,

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:42.840
you need to be more broad-minded,

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:45.460
it can't simply be all about profits,

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.440
and certainly not about profits,

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:51.360
where you are privatizing
gains, and socializing,

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.460
putting on all of us the costs

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.070
of things like your pollution.

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:57.830
So better information makes that possible.

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:00.090
We've got a new capacity with data

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.540
and information technology to track

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:04.530
who's putting those emissions out.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:07.090
And again, in my reimagined world,

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:10.620
making those people pay for
the harms they're causing.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.280
Now, it is also the case

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:15.070
that with better data, and tracking,

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:17.960
we can manage a whole set
of other sustainability

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:19.960
or environmental problems better.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:23.010
And another of the essays
in the "Better Planet" book,

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:27.150
I find really inspiring and
it's a nod to where the future

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:30.190
might go is the one by my
environment school colleague

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:33.750
Marian Chertow on waste management.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:37.010
And Marian, some of you may
know has been in that arena

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:40.580
for something like 30
years, and has really been

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:42.220
on the front lines of thinking

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:46.140
about how we do recapture
and recycling of materials,

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.570
and how we try to minimize waste.

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:52.190
And that's a booming
issue of societal concern,

00:27:52.190 --> 00:27:55.377
obviously around things like
plastic, but even more broadly.

00:27:55.377 --> 00:27:58.810
And so Marian lays out
with one of her students,

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:02.880
a beautiful argument for
a price based strategy

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.450
of waste management,
where you make people pay

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.820
for what they throw away,

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:09.130
but allow them to recycle for free.

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:13.250
And she's got a well structured essay

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:16.160
on how it is we might
reimagine waste management

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.840
to really ensure that we
get to a more closed loop

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.890
use of materials where that
which can be taken back,

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:26.350
recaptured and reused will
be and where we're minimizing

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:29.610
that which goes out forever as waste.

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:31.070
Likewise, there's a great essay

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:33.550
in the book from Tracy Mehan.

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:35.410
And by the way, one of the notable things

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:36.990
about this book is,

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:39.760
these are not essays
from environmentalists,

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:41.690
they're from environmental analysts,

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:45.120
so people who are really
expert in their fields.

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:47.640
And it seeks very much
to have perspectives

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.760
that are not all on the left
side of the political spectrum

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:52.540
but include a number of Republicans,

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:54.700
one of whom is Tracy Mehan,

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:57.880
and has written an excellent
essay on water management.

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:00.460
Basically making the
argument we can't afford

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:02.890
to have such a thing as wastewater,

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:04.930
we need to be able to recapture clean

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:07.620
and reuse all of our water supplies.

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:09.530
Absolutely correct.

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:11.950
This is true in general
even more true in a world

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:13.780
where climate change is gonna stress

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:18.880
traditional water flows,
traditional rainfall patterns.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:21.580
And of course, we're seeing
even in Connecticut this year,

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:23.550
the emergence of a serious drought.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.270
So a great essay on water
as a scarce resource

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:29.840
needing to have a price
on it to incentivize

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:32.310
care and conservation and its management,

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:35.894
and to encourage investments
in the sort of infrastructure

00:29:35.894 --> 00:29:39.910
that allows us to be much more
tight in our water systems,

00:29:39.910 --> 00:29:41.093
not lose water.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:45.750
Now, it also is the case that we recognize

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:49.200
that some of the problems
of the past are a function

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:53.940
of people not thinking
about ecological linkages.

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:58.050
And Schmitz has a great essay
on the need to understand

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:00.280
ecosystems in a more holistic way.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:03.500
Understand the connections
across different aspects

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:05.380
of the plant and animal world.

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:07.820
And I think this provides a very important

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:11.340
science foundation for
this 21st century approach

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:14.040
to environmental protection I'm imagining.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:17.890
And just adjacent to that
argument that Schmitz presents

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:21.730
is another essay from
authors that have their homes

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:23.090
in the school of the environment,

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.270
Paul Anastas, and Julie Zimmerman,

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:28.620
who make the case for
a systems perspective

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:31.210
in environmental law and policy.

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:34.170
An integrated approach,
not a siloed approach,

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:38.210
arguing that the 20th century
was often about managing air,

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.330
and water and waste and land
in separate legal structures,

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:44.656
in separate incentive frameworks.

00:30:44.656 --> 00:30:45.810
And it hasn't worked out that well,

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.570
we misunderstood the connections.

00:30:48.570 --> 00:30:50.550
And in some cases, just move pollution

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:52.720
from one form to another.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.630
Most famously, if you put
a scrubber on a smokestack,

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.860
and it pulls the soot out of the stack,

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.290
now you have a solid waste
and you put it in the land,

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.020
now you have a land waste
and then when rainwater

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.610
comes through it, it
takes it into a stream,

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:07.000
and now you've turned
it into a water problem.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:08.510
So that's not a very good approach.

00:31:08.510 --> 00:31:09.790
And that's the kind of thing

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:12.290
that Paul Anastas and Julie Zimmerman

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:16.380
are calling out with their
essay aimed at systems thinking,

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:19.970
systems design, and really a
much more holistic approach

00:31:19.970 --> 00:31:23.350
to how we produce things, what
kinds of products we produce,

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:27.860
and our framework of law
and policy in response.

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:29.530
Similarly, Jane Lubchenco,

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:32.470
who's written a beautiful essay on oceans,

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.030
tells us that we need to get
beyond our past framework

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:37.290
that the ocean was too big,

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.020
and it wouldn't ever be polluted by us

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:41.590
'cause we couldn't affect
it 'cause it was so vast,

00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:43.950
to the more recent view
that the ocean is doomed,

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:47.200
that it's full of plastic
and hopeless to her argument,

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:51.680
that the ocean is central,
fundamental to life on earth, and

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:54.730
is too big not to fix.

00:31:54.730 --> 00:31:57.150
So that is, I think, a
beautiful perspective.

00:31:57.150 --> 00:32:00.750
But her secondary argument
is that we have to understand

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:03.170
that we have people in our ecosystems,

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:07.240
people affect the ocean, and
we needed to manage the ocean,

00:32:07.240 --> 00:32:09.400
and the people that live on the planet

00:32:09.400 --> 00:32:12.400
in a more integrated way,
understanding trade offs,

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:15.430
and recognizing the need
for the ocean to work

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:18.100
as an ecosystem, but also for people.

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:20.600
And again, it's a systems perspective

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:22.923
played out in one particular venue.

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:27.849
A fabulous essay from Sue
Biniaz, who was for many years

00:32:27.849 --> 00:32:30.470
on the State Department's
climate change team,

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:33.130
the lead environmental lawyer
in the State Department,

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:36.390
she has a similar argument when
it comes to climate change.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:39.210
That you can't rely on
the Paris climate change

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:42.480
agreement of 2015 to do
all the work we need to do.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.700
We've gotta bring others whose behavior,

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:49.900
whose incentive structure
shapes choices that determine

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:52.230
whether we have a lot of
greenhouse gases or not.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:55.130
So she would argue in
the international domain,

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:58.010
yes, we have a Framework
Convention on Climate Change

00:32:58.010 --> 00:32:59.560
with a staff that works on it

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:01.720
but we need to pull in the
World Trade Organization

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:02.797
and the World Bank,

00:33:02.797 --> 00:33:05.210
and the International Labor Organization,

00:33:05.210 --> 00:33:08.310
all to be part of a
unified effort to respond

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.530
to the sustainability challenge broadly

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:13.310
to climate change in particular.

00:33:13.310 --> 00:33:14.530
Another area where I think

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:17.920
we've got work to do is on investment.

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:21.130
As I mentioned, one of
the critical shortfalls

00:33:21.130 --> 00:33:23.710
of the 20th century was
not paying attention

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:25.530
where the money would come from

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:27.300
to create a sustainable future

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:30.560
to invest in the infrastructure
that we would need,

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:33.480
clean energy infrastructure and more.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:37.620
Larry Seltzer, who runs
the the Conservation Fund,

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:39.690
another environmental group,

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:42.100
makes a very interesting argument

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:44.770
that if we are gonna get land conservation

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:47.590
done at the scale we
need, and Tom Lovejoy,

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:50.770
in yet another essay tells us
that biodiversity requires us

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:54.760
to be much more protective of
a much wider swath of land.

00:33:54.760 --> 00:33:57.540
Well, Larry Seltzer's piece
of the story is to say,

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:59.410
governments will never do enough,

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:02.220
we need to make sure that
there is private capital

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:05.930
being deployed to be part of
our land conservation effort.

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:09.790
And he talks about land trusts
and other ways of bringing

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:14.260
private funds into our
land conservation agenda,

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:18.060
and really effectively a new
model for how we're going to do

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:21.010
the scale of habitat protection required

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:23.780
for a thriving human population

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:25.763
as well as plant and animal life.

00:34:27.240 --> 00:34:30.860
Another of the essays that I
think is really interesting

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:32.810
or several of the essays relate

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:36.470
to the need for new
approaches to communications.

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:38.550
Tony Leiserowitz in particular

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:43.140
from our Yale Climate Change
Communications project,

00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:46.120
lays out a very interesting set of views

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:49.120
about how Americans see the
problem of climate change,

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:50.860
what motivates them to take action,

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:53.740
even what words resonate with people.

00:34:53.740 --> 00:34:57.520
And I think understanding
how to communicate science

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:59.460
to the policy arena is again,

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:02.040
a terrible breakdown of the
past and something we need

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:05.989
to get much better about
going forward into the future.

00:35:05.989 --> 00:35:07.710
And that brings me to one final essay

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:08.930
that I just wanna mention.

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:13.930
And in a book with 40 essays
that I'm the editor of,

00:35:14.030 --> 00:35:15.630
I'm not allowed to have a favorite,

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:18.530
just as no parent can
have a favorite child.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:22.020
But I do wanna say, the
essay by Thomas Easley

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:25.750
has resonated in a very big and broad way.

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:28.880
His piece is called "Hip
Hop Sustainability".

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:31.360
And it reflects his reality of being

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:33.430
a dean in the school of the environment,

00:35:33.430 --> 00:35:35.500
but also as a hip hop artist.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:38.320
And the case he makes is for bringing

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:40.920
the discussion around sustainability,

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:43.430
and including climate
change, in particular,

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:46.070
to communities that have been
left out of the conversation.

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:50.070
In his case, the black community,
the inner city community,

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:52.900
the world that he talks
to in his hip hop music.

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:55.280
And his argument is that
if you can speak to people

00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:58.160
in their own terms, in
their own way of approaching

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:00.900
a problem, they're much
more likely to engage.

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:03.200
And he is, of course, correct.

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.870
So his ability to broaden
the discussion bring people

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:09.260
into a political dialogue
that they didn't know

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:11.980
they had an important
reason to be part of,

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:14.300
is I think quite stunning.

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:18.200
So let me close just by
saying a few more visions

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:22.310
of the 21st century strategy
for a sustainable future

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:24.363
that the "Better Planet" book lays out.

00:36:25.272 --> 00:36:26.980
We had a very top down approach

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:28.780
to policy in the 20th century,

00:36:28.780 --> 00:36:33.740
we need to have some top down
priorities and targets set

00:36:33.740 --> 00:36:35.920
but with bottom up implementation,

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:37.790
recognizing not all the work will be done

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:39.670
by the federal government,
some will be done

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:41.460
at the state level,
some at the local level.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:42.970
And oh, by the way, some will be done

00:36:42.970 --> 00:36:45.510
at the global level over at all.

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:48.630
We do have the opportunity
to build on information

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:51.930
technologies that weren't
available in the past century.

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:55.687
We do have enormous
potential to bring data

00:36:55.687 --> 00:36:59.610
and data tracking technologies
to bear to give us new tools

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:02.050
and new possibilities
for how to limit harm,

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:06.260
particularly the idea of
tracing and tracking emissions

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:08.720
and putting a price on those.

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:11.690
And frankly, I would argue
that the environmental arena

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:15.420
is curiously unaffected up till now,

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:18.830
by the entire revolution
of information technology.

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:22.550
No other arena of society
that I'm aware of has done

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:26.380
so little to bring information
technology to bear.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:29.630
You know, no baseball
team today picks players

00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:32.230
the way they might have 30 years ago,

00:37:32.230 --> 00:37:34.870
when a scout would be
chewing tobacco and saying,

00:37:34.870 --> 00:37:37.400
well, that guy has good
stuff and this guy doesn't.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:38.690
Now they're data-driven.

00:37:38.690 --> 00:37:42.540
They now measure how hard
people hit, how fast they run,

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:44.420
how quickly they jump.

00:37:44.420 --> 00:37:47.510
So it's a data-driven
world and that same spirit

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:49.530
just hasn't been deeply embedded

00:37:49.530 --> 00:37:51.770
into the environmental world yet

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:54.590
but there is a potential to do it now.

00:37:54.590 --> 00:37:56.360
As I said, we're gonna need to move away

00:37:56.360 --> 00:37:58.520
from government mandates as the core

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:01.720
of our regulatory framework to
government pricing of harms,

00:38:01.720 --> 00:38:03.300
letting people be flexible

00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:05.000
in what they choose to do about that,

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:08.610
but pay fully for every bit
of harm they're causing.

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:11.270
And again, a more integrated
world where we're thinking

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:15.080
in a unified way, an
ecological way about problems.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:17.740
Recognizing connections and trade offs,

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:21.080
and try to be more coherent
in how we manage that.

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:25.430
And, as I said, it's a world
that needs more innovation.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:27.150
To get to a much cleaner future,

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:29.190
we can't rely on the tools of the past,

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:32.210
the frameworks of the past,
the incentives of the past,

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:33.740
we need to move forward.

00:38:33.740 --> 00:38:37.110
And I do think that the
core of the argument

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:40.750
should be this new value,
foundational value,

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:44.580
that takes the right of
breathers ever more seriously.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:47.370
So I don't think it's the
case that society doesn't care

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:50.320
who has the underlying
environmental rights,

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:52.700
I think we need to lodge with all of us

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:54.860
the right to a clean environment,

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:56.650
to make environmental justice

00:38:56.650 --> 00:38:59.370
a more central part of
our policy framework.

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:02.580
And with that, I would argue
we have a new principle

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:05.350
that commits to an end of externalities.

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:07.880
Every polluter either
stops their harm causing

00:39:07.880 --> 00:39:09.590
or pays fully.

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:11.530
So that's a lot to lay on the table.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:14.520
I invite now questions and comments.

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:18.530
I'm often asked whether
I see a promising path,

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:20.090
whether I'm an optimist,

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:24.270
or whether I think all the
shower of bad news makes

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:26.920
me a pessimist and my answer is,

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:30.740
I am somewhat pessimistic
in the short term,

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:31.670
but in the long term,

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:33.360
I think there's lots of opportunities

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:37.050
and as this book demonstrates,
lots of paths forward.

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:39.830
So with that, let me stop
and invite your questions,

00:39:39.830 --> 00:39:41.290
your comments, your pushback

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.163
and let's have a dialogue.

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:51.450
- Thank you so much, Dan,

00:39:51.450 --> 00:39:54.023
for this wonderful talk and,

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:57.303
please feel free to start typing up

00:39:57.303 --> 00:39:59.253
your questions in the chat.

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:02.490
Ty has kindly offered to moderate

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:04.930
and help us handle the questions.

00:40:04.930 --> 00:40:09.070
And if I may use my
provocative as the organizer

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:10.773
to ask the first question.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:13.553
So Dan,

00:40:14.720 --> 00:40:19.600
I see that you are optimistic
at the policy level

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:23.237
in terms of the interventions
that can be done

00:40:23.237 --> 00:40:26.000
and that will be very, very effective.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:28.950
I was just wondering
whether you are optimistic

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:31.930
in terms of at the individual level,

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:36.610
because we are seeing a
rampant denialism of science,

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:38.380
of climate change.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:42.560
So, how do we change people's minds

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:47.300
about being more open and
sort of accepting of the facts

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:50.093
that are unrolling in front of our eyes?

00:40:51.890 --> 00:40:54.240
Yeah, and the connected
question is, do you see

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:56.900
young activists like Greta Thunberg

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:00.963
actually playing an
important role going forward?

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:05.540
How do you see the sort
of the activism side

00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:07.990
of that movement as well?

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:08.823
Thank you.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:14.707
- So Priya, you've raised one
of the most depressing and,

00:41:14.707 --> 00:41:19.050
"could be pessimism inducing
aspects of our current moment,"

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:24.050
which is, how many people
seem uninterested in science,

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:27.940
in data, in rational analysis,

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:32.940
in having policy grounded, in
facts, and empirical analysis?

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:37.450
So it really, it could be a
source of enormous distress.

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:40.380
And I am actually distressed by that.

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:44.580
I think, however, that the
lesson of our COVID-19 moment,

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:47.920
may well be that if you don't
pay attention to all of that,

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:49.090
you suffer.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:51.010
And it's not that somebody else suffers,

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:53.600
you will suffer, your family will suffer.

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:56.750
And there are kind of straightforward ways

00:41:56.750 --> 00:41:59.130
to put your risk into
a much lower category

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:02.410
by wearing a mask and social distancing.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:04.380
And that the people who are doing that

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:06.940
are having a better outcome
than people who are not.

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:08.770
And, of course, there's bad luck people

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:11.330
that were behaving properly
that still get the disease

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:13.900
and risky behavior folks who don't.

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:16.730
But over the broad scope of society,

00:42:16.730 --> 00:42:19.890
I think it's gonna appear
very clear that the people

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:21.400
who are following science,

00:42:21.400 --> 00:42:24.450
following data are gonna come out better.

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:27.380
But I am stunned at our
current political moment

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:29.360
where so many people still,

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:32.020
even if they have faced
personal challenges,

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:35.300
don't seem to act in their public life,

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:37.020
in their political life,

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:40.170
with regard to that very
straightforward logic.

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:43.720
So I think it is worrisome.

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:46.050
I do think that we have activists

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:48.510
who are starting to sharpen our thinking.

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:50.330
And Greta gets great credit

00:42:50.330 --> 00:42:53.450
for having raised the
profile of climate change.

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:55.460
Her school strikes, I
think, have motivated

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:58.690
lots of young people to
think this can't be left

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:02.080
to the folks who are of my
generation, your generation

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:04.570
to solve the problem, we
haven't yet gotten there.

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:08.140
And the young folks I think are willing

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:11.470
to be more creative to try new things.

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:13.410
But I do think, and I don't know

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:15.330
whether this is put on Greta specifically,

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:19.870
but I do think there is also
some challenges for the young.

00:43:19.870 --> 00:43:23.810
And I see this, you know,
in in many contexts,

00:43:23.810 --> 00:43:26.333
but there are trade offs.

00:43:26.333 --> 00:43:29.250
One of the reasons we still
have a pandemic debate on

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:31.080
is because it is

00:43:31.917 --> 00:43:34.780
a terrible thing that
people have lost their jobs.

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:38.170
So there is an economic counterpoint

00:43:38.170 --> 00:43:40.130
to that pandemic that is real

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:44.150
and people have suffered
really serious life losses

00:43:44.150 --> 00:43:47.510
because of lost jobs,
lost economic security.

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:49.890
So I think the fact is that those who care

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:53.210
about the environment have to
acknowledge that most people

00:43:53.210 --> 00:43:57.350
also care about their
family's economic security,

00:43:57.350 --> 00:43:58.860
the ability to have a job.

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:02.230
So how do you balance
jobs in the environment

00:44:02.230 --> 00:44:04.720
it's gotta be taken more seriously.

00:44:04.720 --> 00:44:06.700
And in that regard, you know, Priya,

00:44:06.700 --> 00:44:08.170
I'm gonna end with your question

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:09.670
'cause I think it's the right one.

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:13.250
I think the answer is data
and science and good analysis,

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:17.130
that's how you do balancing,
that's how you make trade offs.

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:19.010
And I don't see any other path forward

00:44:19.010 --> 00:44:21.660
but to dig deeper into the science

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:24.370
and have it emerge even
more fully and forcefully

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:26.543
as the foundation for our decision making.

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:28.433
- Thank you very much.

00:44:28.433 --> 00:44:30.223
- [Ty] A question I
think from Mrs. Franke.

00:44:31.250 --> 00:44:32.770
- Oh, thank you.

00:44:32.770 --> 00:44:35.653
To follow up with Thomas
Easley's thoughts.

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:40.980
Incidentally, his last
name and mine are the same.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:43.103
My maiden name is Easley, so.

00:44:45.830 --> 00:44:48.490
Has any work been done

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:51.560
to help with education

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:55.120
as far as primary and secondary
schools are concerned?

00:44:55.120 --> 00:44:59.480
I know it is an area where our hope may be

00:44:59.480 --> 00:45:00.910
if they are are educated

00:45:00.910 --> 00:45:03.623
along the lines that you're proposing.

00:45:04.490 --> 00:45:07.610
- So I would tell you
that I'm almost everyone

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:09.990
who's thought long and hard
about what it's gonna take

00:45:09.990 --> 00:45:13.300
to get to a sustainable
future sees education,

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:16.540
and, frankly, in generational shifts

00:45:16.540 --> 00:45:21.400
in underlying understanding
and values as foundational.

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:23.840
And I think this is true
in some arenas that we know

00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:25.713
already and can see the evidence.

00:45:27.128 --> 00:45:29.560
The world of recycling didn't exist

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:32.730
when you and I were young,
we had to be retrained

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:34.840
and we're still not
always probably as good

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:36.730
as the younger folks.

00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:40.350
I have to admit to occasionally
throwing a beer can

00:45:40.350 --> 00:45:42.670
in the trash and not in the recycling bin.

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:44.790
Now, my children just think that's absurd.

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:48.990
They would never imagine
throwing away a good aluminum can

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:52.870
that's ready for repurposing
as a new aluminum can.

00:45:52.870 --> 00:45:56.470
So that's an element of
retraining and of education,

00:45:56.470 --> 00:45:58.860
where the young have got
it clear in their minds,

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.150
they don't even have to think about it,

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:03.000
where some of us are
still slow on the draw.

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:05.760
So I do think, better
environmental education

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:08.130
beginning at the earliest grades,

00:46:08.130 --> 00:46:11.620
deepening the understanding
of science as the foundation

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:15.233
is gonna be quite fundamental
to success on all of this.

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:18.750
- Dan, a question from the chat,

00:46:18.750 --> 00:46:20.363
we have a couple of piled up.

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:23.460
Someone says thanks for raising the issue

00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:27.540
of incorporating externalities
in our economic analysis.

00:46:27.540 --> 00:46:29.423
How do you suggest that might occur?

00:46:30.620 --> 00:46:33.370
- So I think there are
several ways to do this.

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:36.610
Again, I'm a big believer in
bringing a much richer base

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:40.360
of data and analytics
to environmental policy.

00:46:40.360 --> 00:46:42.780
So I would like to have people report

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:45.690
more fully on their environmental impacts.

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:49.890
I think we should require
companies to provide metrics

00:46:49.890 --> 00:46:51.140
that tell us how much in the way

00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:52.700
of greenhouse gases they're emitting?

00:46:52.700 --> 00:46:55.040
What kind of harm they're
putting out their effluent pipe

00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:57.880
into the rivers and streams nearby?

00:46:57.880 --> 00:46:59.300
What they're putting up a smokestack

00:46:59.300 --> 00:47:03.930
beyond greenhouse gases that
might be of societal concern?

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:08.790
And with that data, one can
begin to put a number on it.

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:11.810
That's why I'm saying we
need to recast our EPA

00:47:11.810 --> 00:47:14.360
away from the engineers of the past,

00:47:14.360 --> 00:47:16.940
and toward epidemiologists, ecologists,

00:47:16.940 --> 00:47:19.940
risk analysts and economists,

00:47:19.940 --> 00:47:22.310
who will do that work of putting a number

00:47:22.310 --> 00:47:25.250
on the externalities and
then sending out the bills,

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:27.720
making people pay for
the harm they're causing.

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:31.090
And by the way, some people
think that sounds quite radical,

00:47:31.090 --> 00:47:33.080
it's actually not.

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:35.860
This has been the core
of environmental efforts

00:47:35.860 --> 00:47:39.750
over 400 years, which basically said,

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:44.750
going back to some of the
earliest nuisance cases,

00:47:44.910 --> 00:47:49.110
in British law 400 years ago
on which American law builds,

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:53.370
the most famous case is called Eldridge.

00:47:53.370 --> 00:47:55.730
And in that case, a pig farmer was told

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:58.530
you can't have your smelly pigs
polluting the neighborhood,

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:00.040
stinking up the neighborhood,

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:02.410
either get rid of your pig, stop the harm,

00:48:02.410 --> 00:48:03.810
or you're gonna have to pay your neighbors

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:05.250
for the harm you're causing.

00:48:05.250 --> 00:48:07.680
So this idea of ending externalities,

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:11.660
making people pay for
their harm is 400 years old

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:13.783
in our Anglo-American Legal Tradition.

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:19.210
- Many companies are producing ESG goals

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:22.120
spontaneously from within the organization

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:25.700
but U.S. fossil fuel companies
are singularly refractory

00:48:25.700 --> 00:48:28.693
to such endeavors, how can
they be brought around?

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:33.950
- So I actually am not very optimistic

00:48:33.950 --> 00:48:38.540
about voluntary environmental,
social, governance reporting.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:39.930
I think there's a problem,

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:41.700
never mind in the fossil fuel industry

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:44.480
but broadly that companies tend to report

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:46.890
on their own terms and without regard

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:49.860
to the need for comparability
across the data,

00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:51.630
one company to the next.

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:53.900
So I'm in favor of having

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:56.440
our Securities and Exchange Commission

00:48:56.440 --> 00:48:59.950
establish a core set of
sustainability metrics, again,

00:48:59.950 --> 00:49:02.810
in the environment, social
and governance categories,

00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:07.090
that would be required
alongside of financial data

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:10.920
as part of quarterly and
annually corporate reports,

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:13.610
as providing the foundational information

00:49:13.610 --> 00:49:16.990
that the stock market needs
so that people can understand

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:19.200
who's in a good position, who's not.

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.830
And frankly, it should gauge

00:49:21.830 --> 00:49:24.280
those who are providing
solutions to the market

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:25.840
so that we can say that's a company

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:28.330
that may be a winner over time,

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:30.160
as well as those who are causing harms

00:49:30.160 --> 00:49:33.290
or holding assets that are a problem,

00:49:33.290 --> 00:49:35.700
holding fossil fuel assets, for example.

00:49:35.700 --> 00:49:40.200
So I do think a framework,
a government structure

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:43.530
of sustainability metrics
is probably the way to go.

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:45.930
And that would, of course,
apply first and foremost,

00:49:45.930 --> 00:49:49.270
to the fossil fuel companies
that would then have to tell us

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:52.250
what their risks are, and
whether they're counting

00:49:52.250 --> 00:49:55.100
on getting out all these
fossil fuels they hold

00:49:55.100 --> 00:49:56.513
for their future success.

00:49:57.460 --> 00:49:58.544
- Thank you.

00:49:58.544 --> 00:49:59.377
Another question,

00:49:59.377 --> 00:50:02.680
there are some consequences
to removing fossil fuels.

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:05.490
The LWCF just got full funding

00:50:05.490 --> 00:50:07.550
in the Great American Outdoors Act

00:50:07.550 --> 00:50:09.480
but it is funded by royalties

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:12.840
from oil and gas exploration
in the North Sea.

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:13.920
Won't they have less money

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:16.003
to pay their royalties going forward?

00:50:16.907 --> 00:50:20.110
- Yes and in fact, that
might be an argument in,

00:50:20.110 --> 00:50:22.050
some might push back on my entire pay

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:25.290
for your externalities line of reasoning,

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:28.070
because the goal over time is
actually not to have people

00:50:28.070 --> 00:50:31.130
pay, it's to have people stop polluting.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:33.980
And likewise, to the extent that royalties

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:37.190
from fossil fuel
exploitation are what fund

00:50:37.190 --> 00:50:41.523
that land and water account,
we probably over time

00:50:41.523 --> 00:50:44.880
have to plan for a day when
those numbers begin to dwindle,

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:47.220
because the amount of
fossil fuels being extracted

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:48.923
is going dramatically down.

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:52.640
- Okay.

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:55.130
Please, could you explain
more about the metrics

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:58.700
that companies need to
have to achieve SDG?

00:50:58.700 --> 00:51:01.320
For example, B Lab provides some tools,

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:02.420
but maybe there are more

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:04.910
that can be built in the near future.

00:51:04.910 --> 00:51:07.980
- So this raises another very good point.

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:09.420
And let me just back up in case

00:51:09.420 --> 00:51:12.750
not everyone's familiar
with the SDG reference.

00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:15.480
But in addition, in 2015,

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:18.370
to having the Paris
climate change agreement,

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:20.060
the UN put forward something

00:51:20.060 --> 00:51:22.780
called the Sustainable Development Goals.

00:51:22.780 --> 00:51:25.170
17 different goals that represent

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:28.210
really not just challenges of environment,

00:51:28.210 --> 00:51:30.640
but many other issues,
including ending poverty,

00:51:30.640 --> 00:51:33.270
ending hunger, moving
towards clean energy,

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:35.620
providing better public health,

00:51:35.620 --> 00:51:38.590
all of which are underpinned by metrics.

00:51:38.590 --> 00:51:42.460
So there's 169 targets that have been set,

00:51:42.460 --> 00:51:44.640
that these goals are
meant to drive countries

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:48.300
towards addressing over
the 15 years to 2030.

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:50.650
So I do think there's a
lot of work being done

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:53.210
on measuring Sustainable
Development Goals.

00:51:53.210 --> 00:51:55.950
And a good number of companies,
I would say many companies

00:51:55.950 --> 00:51:59.040
that are serious about their
sustainability strategies

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:02.460
are trying to translate
some of those goals

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:04.640
into their own corporate
reporting framework.

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:08.110
So they're putting
together a corporate report

00:52:08.110 --> 00:52:10.650
on some of the goals that
they think are most relevant

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:12.550
to their own business,
to their own industry,

00:52:12.550 --> 00:52:15.180
and putting that information
out to the world.

00:52:15.180 --> 00:52:17.090
It may well be that as part

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:21.340
of the government mandated ESG disclosure,

00:52:21.340 --> 00:52:23.680
ESG reporting that I would like to see

00:52:23.680 --> 00:52:27.140
that a core set of those
Sustainable Development Goals

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:29.123
would be among the things required.

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:35.613
- Okay, thank you.

00:52:37.540 --> 00:52:40.430
Comments here, the call for bottom up

00:52:40.430 --> 00:52:43.770
or top down is still too anthropocentric,

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:45.470
this is a recipe for collapse,"

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:47.690
one of our participants suggests.

00:52:47.690 --> 00:52:51.670
we need to transcend this
vocabulary and these metaphors.

00:52:51.670 --> 00:52:53.810
He followed up a bit later with this,

00:52:53.810 --> 00:52:57.550
more data and greater amounts
of science are not enough.

00:52:57.550 --> 00:52:59.960
We need to change the
fundamental metaphors

00:52:59.960 --> 00:53:02.120
our culture is working with.

00:53:02.120 --> 00:53:06.000
And he mentions, the fatal consequences

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:09.783
of a misplaced metaphor in
the agricultural industry.

00:53:10.900 --> 00:53:14.890
- So first of all, I do think
how you frame things matters.

00:53:14.890 --> 00:53:17.010
And again, several of the essays

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:19.160
in the "Better Planet"
book make that point.

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:21.840
Jane Lubchenco's essay on the oceans

00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:24.700
is all about a new
narrative for the oceans.

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:26.910
Tony Leiserowitz's work on climate change

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:29.980
is all about how you frame
the issue in a manner

00:53:29.980 --> 00:53:31.750
that engages more people and brings

00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:33.350
them into the conversation,

00:53:33.350 --> 00:53:36.250
gets them to take the
issue more seriously.

00:53:36.250 --> 00:53:38.870
I think that the challenge
that the questioner

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:43.870
is presenting, is that it's
very, very difficult to deliver

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:45.900
such a radical change of perspectives

00:53:45.900 --> 00:53:49.180
that people are going beyond
their own human interest

00:53:49.180 --> 00:53:52.780
to really focus as much as
maybe we would like them to

00:53:52.780 --> 00:53:56.510
on biodiversity, on the world of nature

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:58.970
as an equally important priority.

00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:01.890
And, my own experience in the policy arena

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:05.490
is that it's an in fact a
failing cause to try to hope

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:07.320
that everyone's gonna be high-minded,

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:09.940
and take nature as
seriously as human life.

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:12.370
So instead, I believe that the best hope

00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:16.500
is to frame the agenda around
how nature supports people,

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:18.880
and that we need to protect biodiversity

00:54:18.880 --> 00:54:22.470
and support the ocean,
because in part, at least,

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:25.433
it is essential for our own human success.

00:54:27.060 --> 00:54:27.893
- Thank you.

00:54:27.893 --> 00:54:29.320
Please, could you elaborate more

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:33.180
about environmental justice,
especially because nowadays,

00:54:33.180 --> 00:54:35.760
there is an increased
role of climate litigation

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:39.923
in advancing and undermining
climate change action.

00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:44.800
- So I do think at the core of my vision

00:54:44.800 --> 00:54:47.570
for a 21st century approach
to environmental prediction

00:54:47.570 --> 00:54:51.520
is this idea of not
permitting any externalities.

00:54:51.520 --> 00:54:54.840
That is to say at least
no pollution for free.

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:58.030
There may be some
unavoidable level of of harm

00:54:58.030 --> 00:55:01.208
that's gonna be emitted, but
it should all be fully paid for

00:55:01.208 --> 00:55:04.990
at rates that will provide
ongoing deterrence and encourage

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:08.840
people to continue to move
away from harm causing.

00:55:08.840 --> 00:55:12.000
And I do think more broadly,
that what is now often talked

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:15.120
about as climate justice
beyond environmental justice

00:55:15.120 --> 00:55:20.060
is suggesting that it's unfair
to a number of communities

00:55:20.060 --> 00:55:23.060
to have the risk of climate change rising.

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:25.080
And I like to say that climate change

00:55:25.080 --> 00:55:26.760
is particularly challenging,

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:30.490
because the vectors of responsibility

00:55:30.490 --> 00:55:33.090
are even more complicated
than with most issues,

00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:36.590
which is to say, greenhouse gas
emissions blanket the earth,

00:55:36.590 --> 00:55:40.420
so there is a geographic
spread of enormous scale.

00:55:40.420 --> 00:55:42.910
No one country can
solve the problem alone.

00:55:42.910 --> 00:55:46.340
Even if the United States
entirely counter factually

00:55:46.340 --> 00:55:48.310
were to stop all emissions tomorrow,

00:55:48.310 --> 00:55:50.520
it would only take 15% of the emissions

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:52.470
at the global scale out of the system,

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:54.620
we'd still have 85% remaining.

00:55:54.620 --> 00:55:58.770
So it can only be addressed in
a collective global approach.

00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:01.850
And furthermore, from a point
of view of climate justice,

00:56:01.850 --> 00:56:04.570
there is not only a spatial
spread that's enormous,

00:56:04.570 --> 00:56:07.480
but across time, a temporal spread.

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:10.510
And a lot of the harm
that's being done is to,

00:56:10.510 --> 00:56:12.620
not anyone is alive
today, but our children,

00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:15.130
our grandchildren, and their
children and grandchildren.

00:56:15.130 --> 00:56:18.540
So there is a profound intergenerational

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:20.670
equity dimension here as well.

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:23.600
So I do think environmental
justice is a big topic

00:56:23.600 --> 00:56:26.660
along several different lines, including

00:56:26.660 --> 00:56:28.740
a more immediate harm
spilled from factories

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:30.930
onto adjacent communities,
often communities

00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:33.220
that are disadvantaged,
communities of color,

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:36.310
and then these various vectors of justice

00:56:36.310 --> 00:56:38.300
related to the climate change problem.

00:56:38.300 --> 00:56:39.910
So lots to work through.

00:56:39.910 --> 00:56:43.560
And much of it's been under
attended to in the past

00:56:43.560 --> 00:56:45.480
kind of environmental discussions

00:56:45.480 --> 00:56:48.823
and environmental policy
frameworks that have been in place.

00:56:50.170 --> 00:56:51.550
- Okay, I hope I'm not missing

00:56:51.550 --> 00:56:53.720
the beginning part of this question.

00:56:53.720 --> 00:56:56.960
Wouldn't this additional layer
of sustainability metrics

00:56:56.960 --> 00:56:59.800
result in another level
of corporate governance

00:57:00.804 --> 00:57:01.904
and middle management?

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:07.100
- No, I think the requirement
that companies report

00:57:07.100 --> 00:57:09.410
on the environment is
something that most companies

00:57:09.410 --> 00:57:11.500
of any scale are doing already,

00:57:11.500 --> 00:57:14.800
they're just not doing it
in a coherent cross company,

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:17.100
cross industry, comparable way.

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:21.280
So I think it could, in fact,
be simplifying for companies

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:23.410
to have a single reporting framework

00:57:23.410 --> 00:57:24.830
defined by the government.

00:57:24.830 --> 00:57:28.570
Right now, there's a dozen
different private sector groups

00:57:28.570 --> 00:57:30.590
out there in the data
analytics world trying

00:57:30.590 --> 00:57:33.420
to collect this data, sending out surveys,

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:36.330
pushing companies to
respond to their survey,

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:40.130
and as a result, there's a
scramble within companies

00:57:40.130 --> 00:57:42.070
to keep up with all these requests.

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:45.170
So a single unified government
structure would I think,

00:57:45.170 --> 00:57:48.210
actually cut the amount of
time and effort required,

00:57:48.210 --> 00:57:51.310
and improve the quality and
comparability of the data

00:57:51.310 --> 00:57:53.990
in a way that would benefit
maybe not every company,

00:57:53.990 --> 00:57:55.890
but certainly all the investors

00:57:55.890 --> 00:57:59.430
who want that framework of
comparable, high quality,

00:57:59.430 --> 00:58:02.540
high integrity data that
they can actually trust

00:58:02.540 --> 00:58:04.680
and would view as a foundation

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:06.943
for making investment decisions.

00:58:11.592 --> 00:58:12.425
- Okay.

00:58:12.425 --> 00:58:14.857
Continued comment from
one of the participants.

00:58:14.857 --> 00:58:17.740
"We now need to move beyond
the major universities

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:20.840
and change public discourse in culture.

00:58:20.840 --> 00:58:22.070
Other Rhodes scholars

00:58:22.070 --> 00:58:24.340
have been working on this for a while.

00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:27.427
And he suggests, "I guess
you're familiar with them."

00:58:28.980 --> 00:58:31.380
- So what is the question there?

00:58:31.380 --> 00:58:32.610
- I guess just the comment

00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:34.960
about moving beyond the major universities

00:58:34.960 --> 00:58:39.363
and changing public discourse,
maybe more of just a common.

00:58:39.363 --> 00:58:44.363
- I think there is obviously a
case to have the conversation

00:58:44.590 --> 00:58:46.590
carried beyond universities to be taken

00:58:46.590 --> 00:58:49.630
into the public domain,
into the political arena.

00:58:49.630 --> 00:58:51.990
I think, again, as I mentioned earlier,

00:58:51.990 --> 00:58:55.040
what's notable about this
year's presidential election

00:58:55.040 --> 00:58:57.390
is the degree to which
environment broadly,

00:58:57.390 --> 00:58:59.070
but climate change in particular,

00:58:59.070 --> 00:59:02.750
has been part of the debate,
much more so in the past.

00:59:02.750 --> 00:59:04.480
And I do think, again, we can credit

00:59:04.480 --> 00:59:08.566
some of the younger activists
for having driven the agenda,

00:59:08.566 --> 00:59:10.780
and for having ensured that the candidates

00:59:10.780 --> 00:59:13.570
really in both parties
are having to step up

00:59:13.570 --> 00:59:15.130
and answer questions.

00:59:15.130 --> 00:59:18.980
And I think that's given
the public as clear a signal

00:59:18.980 --> 00:59:22.050
and as sharply defined a
choice on that set of issues

00:59:22.050 --> 00:59:24.553
as we have seen in any recent election.

00:59:30.340 --> 00:59:31.173
- Thank you.

00:59:31.173 --> 00:59:32.470
I think we've covered the questions

00:59:32.470 --> 00:59:35.320
that have been submitted thus far.

00:59:35.320 --> 00:59:37.300
I'm just checking for any new ones,

00:59:37.300 --> 00:59:39.403
but I think that's pretty much it.

00:59:40.320 --> 00:59:41.405
- Good.

00:59:41.405 --> 00:59:44.210
Well, it's been a pleasure to
be part of this conversation.

00:59:44.210 --> 00:59:47.730
I thank Priya and the entire
team at the Franke center

00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:50.770
for the opportunity to be

00:59:50.770 --> 00:59:53.410
with you today and to
have this discussion.

00:59:53.410 --> 00:59:56.670
And hope some of you will carry
it forward in your own way

00:59:56.670 --> 01:00:00.960
and in your own settings in
the weeks and months ahead.

01:00:00.960 --> 01:00:03.470
- Thank you so much Dan for speaking

01:00:03.470 --> 01:00:06.083
and for an absolutely wonderful talk.

01:00:07.000 --> 01:00:09.630
- Well, it's a pleasure
and I wish you all well

01:00:09.630 --> 01:00:11.190
as you think about how to advance

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:14.093
this sustainability agenda
from your own starting points.

